1.0 Competition Description and Objective
The goal of the programming competition is to encourage engineering students to produce a piece of readable software. The teams will use their software development and project management skills to design a solution to a posed problem. This solution will then be presented to "company executives" (the judging panel) for approval. The winning solution will not necessarily be the most technically correct but the one that has the most real-world application and is most thoroughly thought out.


The presentation should highlight the layout, design process, management process and the development process. Components missing due to time constraints must be highlighted, and mistakes should be discussed along with plans to mitigate such problems.
1.1 Team Composition and Eligibility
* Team Size: Must consist of exactly 4 Members
* Eligibility: All Competitors must be enrolled in a York Undergraduate Program. All competitors and teams must also meet the requirements outlined by the Lassonde Engineering Society.
2.0 Competition Details
2.1 Resources
Competitors will present to a panel of judges in person at York University.
Competitors are permitted to use the internet, and if the solution uses any open-source libraries, they must be credited in the presentation.
2.2 Competition Personnel
* The Competition Lead(s): Responsible for the implementation of the competition. The Competition Lead must be present at all of the presentations and be available to competitors and judges at any time for questions and requests.
* Competition Director(s): Responsible for supporting the Competition Lead during the implementation of the competition. The Directors must be available to competitors and judges for questions and requests.
* Official Timekeeper: Responsible for enforcing time limits during the presentations.
* Judges: A minimum of three (3) judges are required to assess the problem-solving abilities, proposed solution, communication skills and team dynamics of the competitors. Judges in this category may come from a variety of backgrounds, including communications, sales and technical or software engineering experience; however, they must have programming experience.
2.3 Competitor Deliverables
Submission: Each team is required to submit, electronically, all the files required to present to the judges before the end of the development period.


The files are to be submitted to this form using the naming conventions mentioned below.


Any team experiencing technical difficulties is permitted to send the appropriately named files by email to yec@lasengsoc.com. The subject line should contain your team name enclosed in square braces, like the following example: "[TeamName] - YEC Programming Submission".


Files you must submit:


1. All presentations must be in PowerPoint or PDF format. Google Doc links are not acceptable and will not be submitted for judging.
2. File naming convention: [TeamName] - SlideDeck - YEC Programming Submission
3. The program files and any additional files mentioned in the challenge. All program files should be submitted in one zipped folder or as one GitHub repo link.
4. File naming convention: [TeamName] - Code - YEC Programming Submission
2.3.1 Technical Details
* Timekeeping: Judges can ask a question at any time during the presentation. The clock will be stopped during these interruptions. To ensure all competitors stop working on the case solutions once the design time ends, competitors may not include any material in their oral presentation that is not included in their submitted presentation materials.
* Plagiarism: Since the use of the Internet and other external resources is permitted in this competition, all information used by competitors must be referenced very carefully. Competitors are not permitted to submit work completed by anyone other than the members of their team. If they decide to recycle their own or someone else's code, it must be clearly cited in the presentation. In addition, competitors must clearly explain why and where they used recycled code in their software. The judges have the right to ask any team member to describe what a particular section of the code does at any given point during the presentation. If there is any evidence that competitors are submitting plagiarized work, the entire team will be eliminated from the competition.
