4.0 Challenge
Problem Statement
Charging electric vehicles (EVs) is a real Ontario issue. A parking structure with chargers can only draw so much power at once, while the price of electricity, and the CO2 released to produce it, change every hour. Charging every car the moment it arrives is simple, but it can cost more, emit more, and leave some drivers without the charge they need. This challenge touches electrical, civil, mechanical, environmental and software engineering, and asks you to weigh economic, environmental and social trade-offs. All data you need is free, public, and supplied to you.
Task
You are contracted by the operator of a fictional campus parkade to develop software that schedules EV charging so that it is cheap, low-carbon and fair. Your program reads the day’s chargers, power limit, cars and hourly electricity data, and decides which car charges on which charger, in which hour, at what power. A simple version is doable in one evening; strong teams win by optimizing. 
Inputs and Output
Inputs (CSV):
      * Chargers (count, max kW each) 
      * Building power cap (kW)
      * Cars (id, arrival hour, departure hour, kWh needed, priority flags)
      * Hourly electricity price and CO2 intensity for Ontario. Price comes from IESO’s public hourly data. CO2 intensity is calculated by GDG on Campus York University from
      * IESO’s hourly generator output by fuel type × standard emission factors per fuel.
      * Teams get a ready-to-use CO2 column.
Output contract:
      * Your program must produce schedule.csv with exactly these columns: car_id, charger_id, hour, kW
      * It is produced from input.csv with one command. Any language is fine (Python, Java, MATLAB, etc.).
      * Everything else (UI, extra outputs, reports, features) is up to the team.
      * Partial credit: rows that break a rule count as unmet charging. They don’t zero thewhole schedule.
Tiers
CORE. Must be finishable on Saturday evening. It needs only loops and sorting, no optimization theory, so teams from any discipline can do it.
      * Read input.csv and write a valid schedule.csv.
      * A simple schedule view. A timeline, table, plot or formatted spreadsheet all count.
      * A summary of total cost, CO2 and unmet charging.
OPTIMIZE.
      * Weighting cost against CO2.
      * Handling changes mid-day, e.g. a car arrives early or the power cap drops at 3 PM.
BONUS. A polished dashboard, what-if scenarios, forecasting.
Fairness and Priority
      * Some cars carry flags: accessible permit, on-call staff, visitor. This rulebook does not say how flags should affect priority.
      * One hidden test case has more demand than the parkade can supply, so not every car can be fully charged.
      * Each team picks a priority policy and defends it in its presentation.The scorer reports unmet charging by flag but doesn’t reward any particular policy.
      * There is no single right answer; this is the social-impact part of the challenge.
Scoring
Every schedule gets a combined score (lower is better):
      * combined score = electricity cost ($) + CO2 cost + unmet-charging penalty
      * CO2 is priced at $110 per tonne ($0.11/kg), Canada’s 2026 federal carbon price benchmark.
      * Unmet charging costs $1.00 per kWh not delivered. That is higher than any hourly price in the challenge data, so leaving a car uncharged is never the cheap option.
      * Your combined score on the hidden test(s) is compared against a simple greedy baseline written by GDG on Campus York University. Matching the baseline earns about half of the hidden-test points; beating it earns more (see Rubric).
      * The scoring is objective and reproducible: you can run the same scorer on your own schedules all weekend.
Starter Pack
Released at the opening on Saturday, November 14:
      * Case packet
      * Sample inputs
      * The validator and scorer, which teams can run all weekend hidden_tests.zip (1 to 2 input files), password-locked until 1:30 pm on Day 2
Submission Requirements
Your program must be able to operate on Windows 10 or 11, or macOS, and files must be submitted as an easy to run program. Any dependencies that you may require should be able to be installed by the user with minimal to no effort.
A presentation that explains how your program works at a high level, along with any key features and bugs must be included in your presentation.
All files are submitted as described in Section 2.3 (submission form first, then email, then USB/upload link), using the naming conventions given there.
Presentation
You will have a combined total of 10 minutes to present your program along with your presentation. Your presentation should consist of a high level design of how your code works, why your system should be implemented, and the priority policy your team chose and why. Judges have the ability to interrupt at any time. The judges are then given about 5 minutes to ask any specific questions about your program they may have.
Allowed Resources
Competitors are allowed to use the following equipment:
      * Competitors are restricted to one laptop per team member
      * Competitors are allowed to use any text editor (vim, emacs, Notepad++) or
      * Integrated Development Environment (Eclipse, VS Code, Visual Studio, Jetbrains)
      * Competitors are allowed to use any programming language
      * Fair use of generative AI is allowed, but is restricted to specifically GitHub Copilot in assistance to writing their code. (Refer to https://education.github.com/ and click on “Get Student Benefits”)
      * Competitors are allowed to use any online resources (i.e. API documentation, StackOverflow, etc.)
      * All data needed for the challenge is in the starter pack. Any other free, public data sets may be used if they are cited.
      * Every open-source library and any recycled code must be cited in the presentation (see Section 2.3.1).
      * Everything needed is free to use. No paid APIs, services or GPUs are required.
Use of AI
Teams that use AI/ML in any way must submit an AI-use document (Section 2.3, file 3) listing every prompt used and the lines of code written with AI assistance. Judges evaluate this at their own discretion: for example, whether AI was used to help explain the logic of the code or to write the code. Large amounts of AI-written code will lead to a deduction of points. If a team used no AI, this is not scored and is omitted when judging.
Penalties
      * Submission time is Nov. 15, 2026 at 1:30 pm (Toronto time). The hidden-test output is due at 1:45 pm. Any deliverable received late loses ten (10) points for every five (5) minutes late, up to the fifty (50) maximum for Documents Received After Deadline (see Rubric).
      * A team that does not upload a hidden-test output scores 0 of the 20 Hidden-Test Performance marks.
      * Code must not be plagiarised. Plagiarism, including a hidden-test output that does not
      * match a team’s submitted code, is penalized and, if confirmed, leads to elimination (see Section 2.3.1).
      * All libraries, recycled code and outside data must be cited. Missing citations are penalized.
      * Every team member must be present for the presentation
